==============================================================================
Proof sketch
==============================================================================
Let Q_m be the fixed-base-path tiling cube, and let C(x)=x+1 be
all-free-tile complement.  The blue/black line graph has one line
for each pair {x,C(x)}.

Blue side: grid-reflection-fixed tilings occur only over SC merged
nodes.  In an SC class the non-fixed tilings pair under reflection,
while the total fixed-base tiling count is odd, so the blue endpoint
count in each active node is odd.  With loops counted twice, the
blue multigraph has odd degree at every active node.

Black side: in an SC node, black = odd total - odd blue, hence even.
In an NS merged pair, black = odd class A + odd class B, also even.
Thus the black line graph is Eulerian: a genuine even graph.

The simple merged metagraph forgets the line multiplicity/address.
That projection is exactly where the parity theorem fails.

==============================================================================
Assumption challenge
==============================================================================
Considered vertex sets: merged tournament nodes, tiling-complement
pairs, single-flip edges, even-graph cycle coordinates, and proof
obligation addresses.  The quotient predicate preserved by the line
lift is degree parity of complement-pair endpoints.  The simple
merged graph preserves adjacency but destroys multiplicity and loop
half-edge data.  Challenged assumption: metagraph edges themselves
need not be the right vertices for parity; the lines are.

Tournament Analysis over parity carriers
  Pairwise observable: lexicographic preservation of parity, multiplicity,
  quotient descent, cycle-space meaning, and LRC transfer value.
